00:00:37.300 --> 00:00:38.620
Engineering Team.

00:00:38.620 --> 00:00:42.157
Welcome to the second in the
three part webinar series.

00:00:42.157 --> 00:00:44.740
MARTIN KRONBERG: And I'm Martin
Kronberg, Technical Evangelist

00:00:44.740 --> 00:00:45.910
with Intel.

00:00:45.910 --> 00:00:47.500
So in the previous
webinar, we learned

00:00:47.500 --> 00:00:52.060
how to take all the data
generated by our gateway

00:00:52.060 --> 00:00:53.770
and send it up to GCP.

00:00:53.770 --> 00:00:56.080
And in this webinar, we're
going to show you guys

00:00:56.080 --> 00:00:58.310
how to make that data useful.

00:00:58.310 --> 00:00:59.810
JOSHUA CHA: By the
end of this talk,

00:00:59.810 --> 00:01:02.340
we will have answered
the following questions.

00:01:02.340 --> 00:01:05.410
First, how can we make all this
data coming in from our devices

00:01:05.410 --> 00:01:06.235
useful?

00:01:06.235 --> 00:01:09.070
Now, how do I manage
these devices at scale?

00:01:09.070 --> 00:01:11.260
And lastly, how can
GCP make it easy

00:01:11.260 --> 00:01:14.140
for me to set up my own global,
high-speed data ingestion

00:01:14.140 --> 00:01:15.310
service?

00:01:15.310 --> 00:01:16.900
To answer these
questions, we're going

00:01:16.900 --> 00:01:18.970
to take your data on a journey.

00:01:18.970 --> 00:01:22.420
And the map of the
journey looks like this.

00:01:22.420 --> 00:01:26.110
What you see here is a series
of Google Cloud Services,

00:01:26.110 --> 00:01:28.210
each with its own role to play.

00:01:28.210 --> 00:01:31.420
This pipeline takes your
data from point A to point B.

00:01:31.420 --> 00:01:32.920
In the previous
webinar, we learned

00:01:32.920 --> 00:01:35.530
how to use the
Intel NUC, send data

00:01:35.530 --> 00:01:37.770
into Google Cloud IoT Core.

00:01:37.770 --> 00:01:40.700
That data ends up
in a Pub/Sub queue.

00:01:40.700 --> 00:01:44.470
From the Pub/Sub queue, we'll
use a Cloud Dataflow pipeline

00:01:44.470 --> 00:01:48.040
to take that data from the queue
into a durable storage service

00:01:48.040 --> 00:01:49.930
called BigQuery.

00:01:49.930 --> 00:01:52.360
And from BigQuery, we'll
visualize that data

00:01:52.360 --> 00:01:55.300
using Cloud Data Studio to
create wonderful dashboards

00:01:55.300 --> 00:01:57.082
for your users.

00:01:57.082 --> 00:01:59.290
But before we dive in, let
me throw it back to Martin

00:01:59.290 --> 00:02:01.450
to talk about the
hardware setup.

00:02:01.450 --> 00:02:02.530
MARTIN KRONBERG: Sure.

00:02:02.530 --> 00:02:04.510
So I just want to take
a quick look again

00:02:04.510 --> 00:02:08.283
at how the sensor data is
actually getting into GCP.

00:02:08.283 --> 00:02:10.449
So for this demo, we're
using the sensor data that's

00:02:10.449 --> 00:02:13.700
coming from our gateway here.

00:02:13.700 --> 00:02:16.450
And we have our gateway attached
to an Arduino 101, which

00:02:16.450 --> 00:02:18.050
we're using at the Sensor Hub.

00:02:18.050 --> 00:02:21.010
Attached to this hub are
three environmental sensors--

00:02:21.010 --> 00:02:23.115
temperature, light, and sound.

00:02:23.115 --> 00:02:24.490
In order to read
the sensor data,

00:02:24.490 --> 00:02:28.420
we're using the MRAA and
UPM libraries from Intel.

00:02:28.420 --> 00:02:30.640
We can use a really
simple high-level API

00:02:30.640 --> 00:02:32.740
to connect to the Sensor
Hub, and then read out

00:02:32.740 --> 00:02:34.990
our values from the sensors.

00:02:34.990 --> 00:02:37.060
If you want to learn more
about these libraries

00:02:37.060 --> 00:02:38.890
and how you can use
them, please refer back

00:02:38.890 --> 00:02:40.809
to the first webinar
of the series.

00:02:40.809 --> 00:02:42.850
So Josh, do you want to
tell us a little bit more

00:02:42.850 --> 00:02:44.230
about that data journey?

00:02:44.230 --> 00:02:45.364
JOSHUA CHA: Absolutely.

00:02:50.010 --> 00:02:52.050
Before we begin, I'd like
to take a minute just

00:02:52.050 --> 00:02:54.929
to explain a little bit
about our global network.

00:02:54.929 --> 00:02:56.970
Now, this is important
because your devices could

00:02:56.970 --> 00:02:58.880
be deployed all over the world.

00:02:58.880 --> 00:03:01.560
And this has implications in how
your devices communicate back

00:03:01.560 --> 00:03:02.790
to the Cloud.

00:03:02.790 --> 00:03:06.090
What you're seeing here is
a map of our global network.

00:03:06.090 --> 00:03:08.640
You'll notice we have over
100 peering locations.

00:03:08.640 --> 00:03:10.860
What this means is
that most likely

00:03:10.860 --> 00:03:12.840
we'll have a presence
in the country

00:03:12.840 --> 00:03:15.210
where your devices
are deployed into.

00:03:15.210 --> 00:03:19.170
This also means that with
a single global IP address

00:03:19.170 --> 00:03:23.160
you can send all your data
to that single IP address,

00:03:23.160 --> 00:03:25.170
and Google will
automatically load

00:03:25.170 --> 00:03:27.390
balance that traffic for you.

00:03:27.390 --> 00:03:30.870
So data can come in to
the closest POP, or point

00:03:30.870 --> 00:03:34.880
of presence, ride our
backbone, and into the services

00:03:34.880 --> 00:03:37.530
in the specific region that
service was set up into.

00:03:43.564 --> 00:03:44.980
Let's talk about
the first service

00:03:44.980 --> 00:03:46.330
to explore on our journey.

00:03:46.330 --> 00:03:49.120
And this is Pub/Sub, our
globally-distributed,

00:03:49.120 --> 00:03:51.490
highly-scaled messaging service.

00:03:51.490 --> 00:03:54.160
When data is sent
to Google Cloud IoT,

00:03:54.160 --> 00:03:56.530
it ends up in a Pub/Sub topic.

00:03:56.530 --> 00:03:58.900
Multiple subscribers can
subscribe to these topics

00:03:58.900 --> 00:04:00.604
and retrieve information.

00:04:00.604 --> 00:04:02.020
It's a durable
service, meaning we

00:04:02.020 --> 00:04:05.256
will retain unacknowledged
messages for up to seven days.

00:04:05.256 --> 00:04:06.880
It's a global service,
as well, meaning

00:04:06.880 --> 00:04:09.790
you could write to a
single URL or IP address,

00:04:09.790 --> 00:04:12.580
and Google will handle the
load balancing for you.

00:04:12.580 --> 00:04:15.410
Coupled with either
Dataflow or Cloud Functions,

00:04:15.410 --> 00:04:17.860
Pub/Sub is your on-ramp
to real-time streaming

00:04:17.860 --> 00:04:19.579
into the Cloud.

00:04:19.579 --> 00:04:22.510
Let me demonstrate how easy it
is to set up your own topic.

00:04:22.510 --> 00:04:25.480
I'll also set up a
simple subscription.

00:04:25.480 --> 00:04:28.150
So what you see here is the
Google Cloud Pub/Sub user

00:04:28.150 --> 00:04:29.080
interface.

00:04:29.080 --> 00:04:31.840
To get there, on
your GCP landing page

00:04:31.840 --> 00:04:33.730
you would simply click
on the hamburger menu

00:04:33.730 --> 00:04:36.100
and select Pub/Sub.

00:04:36.100 --> 00:04:39.790
To create a topic, you
would select Create a Topic.

00:04:39.790 --> 00:04:41.340
And then define your own topic.

00:04:41.340 --> 00:04:43.240
In this case, let's
just call it Demo.

00:04:43.240 --> 00:04:45.211
Click on Create.

00:04:45.211 --> 00:04:47.394
And just that easy
you've created a topic.

00:04:47.394 --> 00:04:49.060
But it doesn't really
do anything useful

00:04:49.060 --> 00:04:51.510
until you can create
a subscription.

00:04:51.510 --> 00:04:55.060
To create a subscription,
simply go into the Demo topic

00:04:55.060 --> 00:04:56.090
that you just created.

00:04:56.090 --> 00:04:58.500
And then click on
Create Subscription.

00:04:58.500 --> 00:05:00.180
In this case, we'll
just call it Demo.

00:05:03.464 --> 00:05:04.380
And there you have it.

00:05:04.380 --> 00:05:07.580
You have your first topic
and subscription duo.

00:05:07.580 --> 00:05:09.470
So lets do something
useful with this.

00:05:09.470 --> 00:05:12.320
Now, we've been sending
data from the end point

00:05:12.320 --> 00:05:13.129
here into Pub/Sub.

00:05:13.129 --> 00:05:14.420
Let's see what that looks like.

00:05:20.010 --> 00:05:23.370
And in this case, I will issue
the simple g call command

00:05:23.370 --> 00:05:24.990
to pull data from
that subscription

00:05:24.990 --> 00:05:27.780
or to an existing subscription
I have that received

00:05:27.780 --> 00:05:29.110
the data from the end point.

00:05:32.960 --> 00:05:34.320
And there you go.

00:05:34.320 --> 00:05:37.130
This is the data being
sent from the sensors

00:05:37.130 --> 00:05:40.250
to the NUC through IoT
cloud through Pub/Sub.

00:05:40.250 --> 00:05:42.001
And there you have it
in the subscription.

00:05:42.001 --> 00:05:42.916
MARTIN KRONBERG: Yeah.

00:05:42.916 --> 00:05:44.334
Pretty straightforward.

00:05:50.535 --> 00:05:53.330
You know, Josh, you
talked about scaling up

00:05:53.330 --> 00:05:57.350
your data infrastructure
using the global GCP network.

00:05:57.350 --> 00:05:59.666
And as you scale up the
Cloud in the back-end,

00:05:59.666 --> 00:06:01.790
you're also going to want
to scale up your hardware

00:06:01.790 --> 00:06:04.280
deployments in order to
gather more sensor data,

00:06:04.280 --> 00:06:07.430
be able to enact any commands
that you want to send down.

00:06:07.430 --> 00:06:11.470
So as you do this, having a
gateway is going to be key.

00:06:11.470 --> 00:06:13.610
So the gateway acts
as a central hub

00:06:13.610 --> 00:06:17.480
for all of the sensors and
actuators in a local area.

00:06:17.480 --> 00:06:20.810
You can send the data gathered
from any number of sensors

00:06:20.810 --> 00:06:22.592
using any kind of interface.

00:06:22.592 --> 00:06:24.050
For instance, if
you're scaling out

00:06:24.050 --> 00:06:27.590
to a factory that has a lot of
legacy sensors, by which I mean

00:06:27.590 --> 00:06:29.840
sensors that do not
have a way of connecting

00:06:29.840 --> 00:06:31.704
to the Cloud
themselves, you're going

00:06:31.704 --> 00:06:33.620
to need a device that
can connect to the Cloud

00:06:33.620 --> 00:06:37.640
and act as a data collection
and communication hub.

00:06:37.640 --> 00:06:39.170
So Intel has gateway
solutions that

00:06:39.170 --> 00:06:43.010
can interface with sensors
over a the local area network,

00:06:43.010 --> 00:06:46.280
or using a serial connection,
like Modbus for Cambus,

00:06:46.280 --> 00:06:50.750
or short range radios like
Bluetooth, ZIGBEE, or ZWAVE,

00:06:50.750 --> 00:06:54.150
as well as many more
connection types.

00:06:54.150 --> 00:06:56.840
So by having a single
connection point to the Cloud

00:06:56.840 --> 00:06:58.340
you're going to
inherently increase

00:06:58.340 --> 00:07:01.420
the security of your deployment.

00:07:01.420 --> 00:07:03.230
You limit possible
points of intrusion.

00:07:03.230 --> 00:07:05.229
You're going to limit the
number of certificates

00:07:05.229 --> 00:07:06.099
you have to manage.

00:07:06.099 --> 00:07:07.640
And you can use the
gateway to set up

00:07:07.640 --> 00:07:10.970
a firewall that only allows
certain protocols through using

00:07:10.970 --> 00:07:12.542
an IP table.

00:07:12.542 --> 00:07:14.000
Overall, it's going
to be much more

00:07:14.000 --> 00:07:18.650
secure than trying to manage
a multitude of connections.

00:07:18.650 --> 00:07:21.440
You could also run some data
preprocessing at the gateway

00:07:21.440 --> 00:07:24.380
before shipping it to the
Cloud, from basic things

00:07:24.380 --> 00:07:27.260
like taking an average over
time to much more complex data

00:07:27.260 --> 00:07:28.329
transformations.

00:07:28.329 --> 00:07:29.870
And I want to come
back to this point

00:07:29.870 --> 00:07:33.200
a little bit later on
with a few examples.

00:07:33.200 --> 00:07:35.370
Finally, as the scale
of your data increases,

00:07:35.370 --> 00:07:39.180
you're going to want to also
make sure that it's consistent.

00:07:39.180 --> 00:07:41.300
So consistent data
is going to be easier

00:07:41.300 --> 00:07:43.560
to parse, as well as to manage.

00:07:43.560 --> 00:07:45.110
And the gateway
can also package up

00:07:45.110 --> 00:07:48.720
that data for consistent
Cloud congestion.

00:07:48.720 --> 00:07:51.980
So in our case, and in many
Cloud connection use cases,

00:07:51.980 --> 00:07:56.220
we're using JSON to send
the data back and forth.

00:07:56.220 --> 00:07:58.730
So this data transfer method
is easy for humans to read.

00:07:58.730 --> 00:08:01.310
And it's easy for
machines to parse.

00:08:01.310 --> 00:08:03.050
It's also quickly
becoming a standard

00:08:03.050 --> 00:08:04.610
for IoT data transport.

00:08:04.610 --> 00:08:07.580
So you'll be able to interface
with many different services

00:08:07.580 --> 00:08:10.810
using the same data pipeline.

00:08:10.810 --> 00:08:13.070
Another aspect of
scaling out your project

00:08:13.070 --> 00:08:16.880
is scaling out the scope of
what the project itself can do.

00:08:16.880 --> 00:08:19.520
So in order to help
developers really expand

00:08:19.520 --> 00:08:21.200
the capabilities
of their projects,

00:08:21.200 --> 00:08:25.310
we have a so-called
path-to-product at Intel.

00:08:25.310 --> 00:08:26.780
What the
path-to-product provides

00:08:26.780 --> 00:08:30.050
is code samples,
proof of concepts,

00:08:30.050 --> 00:08:32.929
as well as market research into
specific verticals in the IoT

00:08:32.929 --> 00:08:35.770
space, including Smart
Home, transportation,

00:08:35.770 --> 00:08:38.534
autonomous driving, so on.

00:08:38.534 --> 00:08:39.409
It's all open source.

00:08:39.409 --> 00:08:42.409
And it's all up on GitHub
under MIT licensing.

00:08:42.409 --> 00:08:45.670
So you can use what
you need from our code.

00:08:45.670 --> 00:08:47.750
Josh, do you want to
tell us a little bit more

00:08:47.750 --> 00:08:50.030
about what the data
can do in BigQuery?

00:08:50.030 --> 00:08:51.098
JOSHUA CHA: Absolutely.

00:08:56.080 --> 00:08:59.290
So BigQuery is my
favorite GCP product.

00:08:59.290 --> 00:09:01.650
Now, in the journey,
you'll see that we're

00:09:01.650 --> 00:09:02.905
going to skip over Dataflow.

00:09:02.905 --> 00:09:04.530
And we'll get back
to that in a second.

00:09:04.530 --> 00:09:06.140
You'll see why.

00:09:06.140 --> 00:09:08.224
For now, let's talk
about BigQuery.

00:09:08.224 --> 00:09:10.140
BigQuery is Google's
analytics database that's

00:09:10.140 --> 00:09:12.210
built for petabyte-scale.

00:09:12.210 --> 00:09:14.007
It's ideal for the
analytics use cases

00:09:14.007 --> 00:09:16.590
because you don't have to worry
about deploying more resources

00:09:16.590 --> 00:09:19.230
when you're constantly
pumping in more IoT data

00:09:19.230 --> 00:09:21.390
from these devices.

00:09:21.390 --> 00:09:24.189
It's fully managed,
No-Ops Data Warehouse.

00:09:24.189 --> 00:09:25.980
It's built for
petabyte-scale as mentioned,

00:09:25.980 --> 00:09:28.290
but it's super fast
at the same time.

00:09:28.290 --> 00:09:29.880
You get the convenience of SQL.

00:09:29.880 --> 00:09:31.950
So if you're used
to writing SQL,

00:09:31.950 --> 00:09:34.440
you'll be able to work
with BigQuery easily.

00:09:34.440 --> 00:09:36.690
And finally, it's an
externalized version

00:09:36.690 --> 00:09:38.940
of Google Dremel, which is
a database we've been using

00:09:38.940 --> 00:09:40.720
internally for over a decade.

00:09:40.720 --> 00:09:41.730
So it's been hardened.

00:09:41.730 --> 00:09:42.606
It's been tested.

00:09:42.606 --> 00:09:43.980
And it's been put
into production

00:09:43.980 --> 00:09:46.746
for well over 10 years now.

00:09:46.746 --> 00:09:48.120
So let me demonstrate
how easy it

00:09:48.120 --> 00:09:51.480
is to set up your own database
and issue some simple commands.

00:09:51.480 --> 00:09:54.270
So the first thing you'll
notice is that you're

00:09:54.270 --> 00:09:55.680
using SQL constructs here.

00:09:55.680 --> 00:09:57.900
So you're going to select
star from this table.

00:09:57.900 --> 00:10:00.585
Order by ascending order.

00:10:00.585 --> 00:10:01.750
Click on Run Create.

00:10:01.750 --> 00:10:03.286
It's going to run the query.

00:10:03.286 --> 00:10:04.410
And notice how fast it was.

00:10:04.410 --> 00:10:07.120
This table has over 7,000 rows.

00:10:07.120 --> 00:10:08.920
Let's try it in
descending order.

00:10:08.920 --> 00:10:11.040
Run the same query to see
the data change a bit.

00:10:11.040 --> 00:10:11.970
And there you go.

00:10:11.970 --> 00:10:12.470
Right?

00:10:12.470 --> 00:10:14.620
So it's going to sort
this in descending order.

00:10:14.620 --> 00:10:16.460
Notice how super fast it was.

00:10:16.460 --> 00:10:20.500
To create a table, again,
click on this down arrow.

00:10:20.500 --> 00:10:21.230
Super fast.

00:10:21.230 --> 00:10:21.810
We're going to create a dataset.

00:10:21.810 --> 00:10:23.770
And we're just going
to call it demo.

00:10:23.770 --> 00:10:25.320
Click on OK.

00:10:25.320 --> 00:10:28.800
And from the demo, you
can create a new table.

00:10:28.800 --> 00:10:30.780
And you can either
create it from source

00:10:30.780 --> 00:10:32.010
or create an empty table.

00:10:32.010 --> 00:10:35.701
Let's just create an empty
table, and call it demo table.

00:10:35.701 --> 00:10:36.450
Keep that in mind.

00:10:36.450 --> 00:10:38.090
We'll get back to
that in a little bit.

00:10:38.090 --> 00:10:39.423
And just add a couple of fields.

00:10:39.423 --> 00:10:42.340
Let's try timestamp,
which is an integer.

00:10:42.340 --> 00:10:47.280
And let's try temperature,
which is a float here.

00:10:47.280 --> 00:10:51.330
So I'm going to click
on Create Table.

00:10:51.330 --> 00:10:53.520
And there you have it,
your first BigQuery table.

00:10:59.220 --> 00:11:01.400
Now that we have data
coming into Pub/Sub,

00:11:01.400 --> 00:11:03.690
and we have a place
to store that data,

00:11:03.690 --> 00:11:07.522
how do we get that data from
the Pub/Sub topic into BigQuery?

00:11:07.522 --> 00:11:09.230
There are a few options,
but today, we'll

00:11:09.230 --> 00:11:11.960
focus on Cloud Dataflow.

00:11:11.960 --> 00:11:13.700
If you are familiar
with Apache Beam,

00:11:13.700 --> 00:11:16.342
then you'll feel right at
home with Cloud Dataflow.

00:11:16.342 --> 00:11:18.050
Cloud Dataflow is a
fully managed service

00:11:18.050 --> 00:11:21.860
for transforming and enriching
data in stream or real-time

00:11:21.860 --> 00:11:24.980
or in batch historical
with equal reliability

00:11:24.980 --> 00:11:26.240
and expressiveness.

00:11:26.240 --> 00:11:29.720
So no more complex workarounds
or compromises needed.

00:11:29.720 --> 00:11:31.490
At a high level,
you define a source,

00:11:31.490 --> 00:11:34.580
which we call inbound data
and a sync, outbound data.

00:11:34.580 --> 00:11:37.170
When you bring data in,
it becomes a collection

00:11:37.170 --> 00:11:39.170
in which a pipeline of
operations, which we call

00:11:39.170 --> 00:11:41.370
transforms, can be performed.

00:11:41.370 --> 00:11:43.910
The data source can be
batch or it can be streamed.

00:11:43.910 --> 00:11:45.710
And there is a time
windowing concept which

00:11:45.710 --> 00:11:47.840
allows for great extensibility.

00:11:47.840 --> 00:11:50.390
You do all of this under a
single programming model,

00:11:50.390 --> 00:11:52.588
which reduces complexity.

00:11:52.588 --> 00:11:53.962
Before we go to
a demo, I'm going

00:11:53.962 --> 00:11:56.128
to throw it back to Martin
to talk a little bit more

00:11:56.128 --> 00:11:56.910
about ETL.

00:11:56.910 --> 00:11:57.826
MARTIN KRONBERG: Sure.

00:11:57.826 --> 00:12:00.530
So there's going to be three
main parts of data handling

00:12:00.530 --> 00:12:02.810
that the gateway does for us--

00:12:02.810 --> 00:12:05.240
the extraction,
transformation, and loading

00:12:05.240 --> 00:12:09.140
of data, or ETL as it's
referred to in data science.

00:12:09.140 --> 00:12:11.060
We discussed the
extraction of data

00:12:11.060 --> 00:12:12.992
or gathering the sensor data.

00:12:12.992 --> 00:12:14.450
We talked about
the loading of data

00:12:14.450 --> 00:12:17.199
or ingesting it into
Google Cloud Platform.

00:12:17.199 --> 00:12:18.740
Now, I want to talk
a little bit more

00:12:18.740 --> 00:12:21.470
about the transformation
of the data.

00:12:21.470 --> 00:12:24.402
So raw data is going
to be a little rough.

00:12:24.402 --> 00:12:25.610
There's going to be outliers.

00:12:25.610 --> 00:12:27.156
There's going to be some noise.

00:12:27.156 --> 00:12:29.030
And there's going to be
a time series of data

00:12:29.030 --> 00:12:31.219
that follows certain trends.

00:12:31.219 --> 00:12:33.260
When we're dealing with
a massive amount of data,

00:12:33.260 --> 00:12:36.080
as a large-scale
industrial deployment will,

00:12:36.080 --> 00:12:40.440
we want to be able to clean that
data up as fast as possible.

00:12:40.440 --> 00:12:42.120
So to facilitate
this process, Intel

00:12:42.120 --> 00:12:45.240
has developed a set of libraries
called the Data Analytics

00:12:45.240 --> 00:12:48.110
Acceleration Libraries or DAAL.

00:12:48.110 --> 00:12:50.720
These libraries cover a vast
amount of data transformation

00:12:50.720 --> 00:12:52.100
methods--

00:12:52.100 --> 00:12:54.380
linear regression,
outlier detection,

00:12:54.380 --> 00:12:58.070
and low order moments, or
things like means, sums,

00:12:58.070 --> 00:12:59.780
standard deviations.

00:12:59.780 --> 00:13:01.940
So these are just a
few of the core tools

00:13:01.940 --> 00:13:05.150
that a data scientist can
use to transform raw data.

00:13:05.150 --> 00:13:07.700
And all of these
tools and many more

00:13:07.700 --> 00:13:11.000
are provided by
the DAAL library.

00:13:11.000 --> 00:13:14.300
And using that you can transform
your raw data into something

00:13:14.300 --> 00:13:17.660
more usable that can then be
fed into our Cloud Analytics

00:13:17.660 --> 00:13:19.227
pipeline.

00:13:19.227 --> 00:13:20.810
So these libraries
have been optimized

00:13:20.810 --> 00:13:22.550
to run on Intel
architecture in order

00:13:22.550 --> 00:13:24.860
to provide an extremely
fast way to transform

00:13:24.860 --> 00:13:26.740
large amounts of data.

00:13:26.740 --> 00:13:31.790
And the best part is that
DAAL runs on Hadoop, Spark, R,

00:13:31.790 --> 00:13:32.737
and Matlab.

00:13:32.737 --> 00:13:34.820
So that means that you can
use the data processing

00:13:34.820 --> 00:13:37.934
pipeline with which you
are most familiar with.

00:13:37.934 --> 00:13:40.100
If you want to try out these
libraries for yourself,

00:13:40.100 --> 00:13:45.620
go to
software.intel.com/Intel-DAAL

00:13:45.620 --> 00:13:47.990
to download the libraries,
read about how to use them,

00:13:47.990 --> 00:13:49.450
and try them out.

00:13:49.450 --> 00:13:51.950
JOSHUA CHA: Now that we have
the ETL performing on the Intel

00:13:51.950 --> 00:13:54.290
device itself, we can
still use Dataflow

00:13:54.290 --> 00:13:58.280
to transfer the data from
Pub/Sub topic into BigQuery.

00:13:58.280 --> 00:13:59.630
Let's see how.

00:13:59.630 --> 00:14:01.100
To get to Cloud Dataflow.

00:14:01.100 --> 00:14:04.790
You click on the hamburger
menu and click on Dataflow.

00:14:04.790 --> 00:14:07.100
In order to have a number
of services already running,

00:14:07.100 --> 00:14:08.990
this particular
pipeline is running

00:14:08.990 --> 00:14:11.630
to take the data from
this device from the topic

00:14:11.630 --> 00:14:13.210
and into BigQuery.

00:14:13.210 --> 00:14:15.140
To create a new
pipeline, I simply

00:14:15.140 --> 00:14:17.489
create a job from template.

00:14:17.489 --> 00:14:19.280
Now, we're using a
template here because we

00:14:19.280 --> 00:14:21.113
don't have to do anything
sophisticated here

00:14:21.113 --> 00:14:23.900
because the NUC here is
going to handle the ETL.

00:14:23.900 --> 00:14:26.764
I give this a name.

00:14:26.764 --> 00:14:28.430
I'm going to use the
third option, which

00:14:28.430 --> 00:14:30.575
is Cloud Pub/Sub to BigQuery.

00:14:30.575 --> 00:14:33.200
And after selecting this option,
I'm going to select two more--

00:14:33.200 --> 00:14:35.033
I need to provide two
more pieces of detail.

00:14:35.033 --> 00:14:37.160
One is the actual topic.

00:14:37.160 --> 00:14:40.280
Since I have my Pub/Sub
topic already opened here,

00:14:40.280 --> 00:14:42.740
I'm going to select this topic.

00:14:45.780 --> 00:14:49.410
And from BigQuery, I'm
going to select this table.

00:14:49.410 --> 00:14:51.990
Now you know why we
skipped over Cloud Dataflow

00:14:51.990 --> 00:14:54.970
and went directly to BigQuery.

00:14:54.970 --> 00:14:55.780
Two

00:14:55.780 --> 00:14:58.400
So now that we have the
BigQuery table defined,

00:14:58.400 --> 00:15:00.540
we click on Run Job.

00:15:00.540 --> 00:15:01.260
And there you go.

00:15:01.260 --> 00:15:04.230
That's your first Cloud
Dataflow pipeline taking data

00:15:04.230 --> 00:15:06.300
from a Pub/Sub topic
and into BigQuery.

00:15:12.255 --> 00:15:14.130
At this point, we've
covered a lot of ground.

00:15:14.130 --> 00:15:18.350
So let's quickly review
what we've done thus far.

00:15:18.350 --> 00:15:19.820
On our data journey,
we started out

00:15:19.820 --> 00:15:23.360
with the sensor information
coming into Cloud IoT Core.

00:15:23.360 --> 00:15:26.870
From there, the data got
into a Pub/Sub topic.

00:15:26.870 --> 00:15:29.420
Just now, we create a
pipeline to take that data

00:15:29.420 --> 00:15:33.320
from the topic
and into BigQuery.

00:15:33.320 --> 00:15:35.660
Now, with the data
in BigQuery, we

00:15:35.660 --> 00:15:37.130
can move on to the
last step, which

00:15:37.130 --> 00:15:39.339
is using Cloud Data Studio,
which is a free tool that

00:15:39.339 --> 00:15:41.629
allows you to make beautiful
charts from your streaming

00:15:41.629 --> 00:15:42.470
data.

00:15:42.470 --> 00:15:44.180
Instead of me talking,
let me walk you

00:15:44.180 --> 00:15:45.260
through a simple example.

00:15:48.640 --> 00:15:50.800
To get to Data
Studio, you simply

00:15:50.800 --> 00:15:53.000
go to DataStudio.Google.com.

00:15:53.000 --> 00:15:55.810
To start, click on
the big blue plus

00:15:55.810 --> 00:15:58.220
button to start a new dashboard.

00:15:58.220 --> 00:16:00.010
The first step when
you create a dashboard

00:16:00.010 --> 00:16:01.510
is to create your
first data source.

00:16:01.510 --> 00:16:04.744
In this case, I'm going to
create one from my GCP project.

00:16:04.744 --> 00:16:06.160
You click on this
big blue button.

00:16:06.160 --> 00:16:08.530
And you'll notice that
Data Studio offers

00:16:08.530 --> 00:16:10.330
a number of data connectors.

00:16:10.330 --> 00:16:12.340
This makes it easy to
connect your data sources.

00:16:12.340 --> 00:16:14.800
In this case, we're
going to use BigQuery.

00:16:14.800 --> 00:16:16.840
And because I've logged
in with my GCP account,

00:16:16.840 --> 00:16:19.450
it knows which projects
I have access to.

00:16:19.450 --> 00:16:22.870
In this case, we'll be
connecting to the Intel webinar

00:16:22.870 --> 00:16:25.780
project as appropriate.

00:16:25.780 --> 00:16:26.590
Click on Edge Data.

00:16:26.590 --> 00:16:29.020
And here's that demo
BigQuery table we created.

00:16:29.020 --> 00:16:31.816
Here's Edge Data, Data Table.

00:16:31.816 --> 00:16:33.190
And I'm going to
click on Connect

00:16:33.190 --> 00:16:36.312
and connect this data source.

00:16:36.312 --> 00:16:39.040
What you'll notice here is
that I didn't code up anything.

00:16:39.040 --> 00:16:42.550
What Data Studio did was looked
into that BigQuery table,

00:16:42.550 --> 00:16:44.050
found the columns,
and automatically

00:16:44.050 --> 00:16:45.690
created these metrics for me.

00:16:45.690 --> 00:16:48.110
It's smart enough to identify
that light was number,

00:16:48.110 --> 00:16:50.260
sound was a number,
temperature is a number.

00:16:50.260 --> 00:16:51.940
Even the timestamp,
which is specified

00:16:51.940 --> 00:16:54.640
as an integer in BigQuery,
it was identified

00:16:54.640 --> 00:16:57.710
as a date, which is correct.

00:16:57.710 --> 00:17:00.260
Here, I'm going to change
just a few parameters around.

00:17:00.260 --> 00:17:04.000
I'm going to make these
averages instead of sums.

00:17:04.000 --> 00:17:06.310
And let's add a bit
more detail to the time

00:17:06.310 --> 00:17:08.410
here to include both
the day and the hour,

00:17:08.410 --> 00:17:10.210
just to make it a
bit more interesting.

00:17:10.210 --> 00:17:12.900
I'm going to add
that to the report.

00:17:12.900 --> 00:17:15.380
And OK?

00:17:15.380 --> 00:17:17.030
Here's your first dashboard.

00:17:17.030 --> 00:17:19.420
Let's create a table that
just takes the raw data

00:17:19.420 --> 00:17:21.190
and dumps it to this screen.

00:17:21.190 --> 00:17:24.160
To do so, I click
on this table icon.

00:17:24.160 --> 00:17:26.043
Draw up the table
in the dashboard.

00:17:29.565 --> 00:17:31.940
And you'll notice immediately
the time stamps are already

00:17:31.940 --> 00:17:32.752
populated here.

00:17:32.752 --> 00:17:35.210
It added light, but we could
add a couple of other metrics.

00:17:35.210 --> 00:17:38.400
And let's add sound.

00:17:38.400 --> 00:17:41.135
And lastly, let's
add temperature.

00:17:44.700 --> 00:17:46.000
And there you go.

00:17:46.000 --> 00:17:48.520
So now you have a data
dump of information

00:17:48.520 --> 00:17:49.805
coming from the sensor.

00:17:49.805 --> 00:17:52.720
But let's create a chart now to
make it a bit more attractive.

00:17:52.720 --> 00:17:54.803
So I'm going to start with
this time series chart.

00:17:54.803 --> 00:17:56.140
It's essentially a line chart.

00:17:56.140 --> 00:17:58.076
I'm going to drag
into the dashboard.

00:17:58.076 --> 00:18:00.670
And you'll notice
that on the x-axis

00:18:00.670 --> 00:18:03.700
it'll automatically populate
it with a time stamp.

00:18:03.700 --> 00:18:05.130
By default, it's
adding the light,

00:18:05.130 --> 00:18:08.610
but we could add the sound.

00:18:08.610 --> 00:18:14.510
And let's also add
the temperature.

00:18:14.510 --> 00:18:15.470
And there you have it.

00:18:15.470 --> 00:18:18.050
In about five minutes we
created a beautiful table

00:18:18.050 --> 00:18:19.970
with all your data
and a chart that

00:18:19.970 --> 00:18:23.417
gives it a bit more meaning
than just a list of raw numbers.

00:18:23.417 --> 00:18:25.250
MARTIN KRONBERG: Yeah,
so this visualization

00:18:25.250 --> 00:18:26.165
looks really great.

00:18:26.165 --> 00:18:28.040
And I'm guessing that
you can easily share it

00:18:28.040 --> 00:18:29.540
with other people in your team.

00:18:29.540 --> 00:18:30.581
JOSHUA CHA: That's right.

00:18:30.581 --> 00:18:32.726
To do so, you just click
on the sharing icon

00:18:32.726 --> 00:18:35.600
and list your teammates that
you want to share it with.

00:18:35.600 --> 00:18:36.382
That's it.

00:18:36.382 --> 00:18:37.340
MARTIN KRONBERG: Great.

00:18:37.340 --> 00:18:38.631
I mean, this looks really good.

00:18:38.631 --> 00:18:40.340
And I can definitely
see how developers

00:18:40.340 --> 00:18:44.336
can use it to start pulling out
business logic from their data.

00:18:44.336 --> 00:18:45.710
Before we sign
off, I just wanted

00:18:45.710 --> 00:18:48.350
to circle back and
discuss a little bit more

00:18:48.350 --> 00:18:50.390
about advanced
analytics that you can

00:18:50.390 --> 00:18:52.890
do on the Intel-based gateways.

00:18:52.890 --> 00:18:55.490
So the type of data that
we're able to visualize here,

00:18:55.490 --> 00:18:58.730
and really in almost all
Cloud-based frameworks,

00:18:58.730 --> 00:19:02.210
is limited to what you can
send via JSON, so numbers,

00:19:02.210 --> 00:19:05.840
like floats or integers,
and strings of characters.

00:19:05.840 --> 00:19:08.450
This works great for
many data applications.

00:19:08.450 --> 00:19:10.400
Our demo here uses
sensor data, which

00:19:10.400 --> 00:19:14.060
is encoded as an integer
value between 0 and 1023,

00:19:14.060 --> 00:19:16.130
which is a pretty standard
way of doing analog

00:19:16.130 --> 00:19:18.100
to digital conversion.

00:19:18.100 --> 00:19:19.850
But what if the data
that you're gathering

00:19:19.850 --> 00:19:22.850
can't be encoded into
a number or a string?

00:19:22.850 --> 00:19:24.860
So for instance, more
and more applications

00:19:24.860 --> 00:19:27.500
are based around
gathering video or images.

00:19:27.500 --> 00:19:29.630
And the only way to send
an image with a JSON

00:19:29.630 --> 00:19:32.960
would be to break it up into
an array of pixel values.

00:19:32.960 --> 00:19:36.410
And this can be both cumbersome
and very bandwidth intensive.

00:19:36.410 --> 00:19:39.260
So what you need to
do then is some kind

00:19:39.260 --> 00:19:41.240
of advanced
analytics at the edge

00:19:41.240 --> 00:19:44.310
before we can send that
data up to the Cloud.

00:19:44.310 --> 00:19:47.510
So Intel provides a whole
host of tools for just that

00:19:47.510 --> 00:19:49.820
with the Intel System Studio.

00:19:49.820 --> 00:19:53.270
This is an IDE for C++ that
integrates a whole host

00:19:53.270 --> 00:19:56.840
of libraries and SDKs, which
are created by Intel and meant

00:19:56.840 --> 00:19:58.970
for advanced analytics.

00:19:58.970 --> 00:20:02.660
Checkout
software.intel.com/system-studio

00:20:02.660 --> 00:20:06.200
to learn more, download the
IDE, and just start using it

00:20:06.200 --> 00:20:08.750
and applying it
to your projects.

00:20:08.750 --> 00:20:10.820
One of the tools that's
included in this package

00:20:10.820 --> 00:20:13.010
is the Computer Vision SDK.

00:20:13.010 --> 00:20:15.290
So this SDK was released
into public beta

00:20:15.290 --> 00:20:17.690
about a month or so
back and integrates

00:20:17.690 --> 00:20:19.940
a bunch of really useful
tools for Computer Vision

00:20:19.940 --> 00:20:21.560
applications.

00:20:21.560 --> 00:20:23.900
It's based around OpenCV,
which is an open source

00:20:23.900 --> 00:20:26.540
project for Computer
Vision, originally developed

00:20:26.540 --> 00:20:29.504
at Intel back in 2000.

00:20:29.504 --> 00:20:30.920
It's grown quite
a bit since then,

00:20:30.920 --> 00:20:34.220
thanks to an extremely
active developer community.

00:20:34.220 --> 00:20:37.580
The CV SDK expands on the
capabilities of OpenCV

00:20:37.580 --> 00:20:41.870
by adding in optimizers, deep
learning, a visual algorithm

00:20:41.870 --> 00:20:44.090
designer, and a lot more tools.

00:20:44.090 --> 00:20:48.050
And of course, it's been
optimized to run on Intel CPUs.

00:20:48.050 --> 00:20:51.410
So what does this mean
in terms of our Dataflow?

00:20:51.410 --> 00:20:54.620
Well, it means that instead
of sending up an entire image,

00:20:54.620 --> 00:20:58.070
you can analyze it with
the CV SDK on the gateway,

00:20:58.070 --> 00:21:01.910
and then send a data format
that's easy for our pipeline

00:21:01.910 --> 00:21:03.220
to ingest.

00:21:03.220 --> 00:21:05.300
And in this way you can
expand the kinds of data

00:21:05.300 --> 00:21:07.361
that you can use in
your application.

00:21:12.422 --> 00:21:14.130
JOSHUA CHA: That's
all we have for today.

00:21:14.130 --> 00:21:15.546
We hope that today's
session shows

00:21:15.546 --> 00:21:18.230
that with the combined power
of Intel's hardware platforms

00:21:18.230 --> 00:21:20.270
with Google Cloud
you too can build

00:21:20.270 --> 00:21:22.557
a highly scalable,
reliable data pipeline.

00:21:22.557 --> 00:21:24.140
MARTIN KRONBERG: And
please stay tuned

00:21:24.140 --> 00:21:25.490
for our next webinar
in which we're

00:21:25.490 --> 00:21:26.990
going to demonstrate
how you can use

00:21:26.990 --> 00:21:30.319
this data to build your own
machine learning solutions.

00:21:30.319 --> 00:21:30.860
Thanks, guys.

00:21:30.860 --> 00:21:33.340
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:21:56.469 --> 00:21:57.510
JOSHUA CHA: Welcome back.

00:21:57.510 --> 00:21:59.210
We hope you enjoyed
the presentation.

00:21:59.210 --> 00:22:00.810
And thank you for
your questions.

00:22:00.810 --> 00:22:02.310
We've gathered a
number of questions

00:22:02.310 --> 00:22:03.642
that we hope we can answer.

00:22:03.642 --> 00:22:05.600
To start off, let's start
with number one here.

00:22:05.600 --> 00:22:06.990
How do you charge for BigQuery?

00:22:06.990 --> 00:22:10.460
Query Now, there are three
dimensions to BigQuery pricing.

00:22:10.460 --> 00:22:11.600
The first one is storage.

00:22:11.600 --> 00:22:12.766
The second one is streaming.

00:22:12.766 --> 00:22:14.750
And the third is the query.

00:22:14.750 --> 00:22:16.760
On the storage side,
we charge about $0.02

00:22:16.760 --> 00:22:18.720
per gigabyte per month.

00:22:18.720 --> 00:22:20.700
On the streaming side,
we only charge a cent

00:22:20.700 --> 00:22:22.580
per 200 megabytes per month.

00:22:22.580 --> 00:22:27.380
And on the query side, it's
$5 per terabyte of query,

00:22:27.380 --> 00:22:29.780
with the first
terabyte being free.

00:22:29.780 --> 00:22:33.710
Now, data coming into BigQuery
and data coming out of BigQuery

00:22:33.710 --> 00:22:34.290
is free.

00:22:34.290 --> 00:22:36.740
So if you have any
questions, there's

00:22:36.740 --> 00:22:39.865
more information on the website
under BigQuery in the Google

00:22:39.865 --> 00:22:42.140
Cloud platform website.

00:22:42.140 --> 00:22:45.350
Number two, another
BigQuery related question.

00:22:45.350 --> 00:22:47.611
You only showed
BigQuery using a UI.

00:22:47.611 --> 00:22:49.610
Is that the only way to
interface with BigQuery.

00:22:49.610 --> 00:22:52.590
The answer is no,
there's multiple ways.

00:22:52.590 --> 00:22:56.610
The first way is through our
REST APIs, very well-known

00:22:56.610 --> 00:22:57.890
REST APIs.

00:22:57.890 --> 00:23:01.105
The second way is through out
G Cloud command line interface.

00:23:01.105 --> 00:23:03.230
The third way, and I would
say the most popular way

00:23:03.230 --> 00:23:05.200
people are interfacing
with BigQuery,

00:23:05.200 --> 00:23:06.620
is through our native APIs.

00:23:06.620 --> 00:23:11.510
So we do have APIs built
for Python, .NET, and Java.

00:23:11.510 --> 00:23:14.120
So as a developer,
you have your options

00:23:14.120 --> 00:23:17.472
on how you want to leverage
BigQuery into your project.

00:23:17.472 --> 00:23:19.260
SUNDAR SUBRAMANIAM: Cool.

00:23:19.260 --> 00:23:21.740
Here's a question
on sensor libraries.

00:23:21.740 --> 00:23:23.690
So the question is,
how do I learn more

00:23:23.690 --> 00:23:27.470
about sensor libraries
used on the Intel device?

00:23:27.470 --> 00:23:33.200
So Intel actually maintains a
website called the upm.mraa.io.

00:23:33.200 --> 00:23:37.800
It has all the information about
the sensor libraries, the APIs,

00:23:37.800 --> 00:23:40.520
which sensors you could use.

00:23:40.520 --> 00:23:43.460
This site was recently
revamped, so you

00:23:43.460 --> 00:23:46.710
should be able to access
all this information.

00:23:46.710 --> 00:23:48.290
It should be easily accessible.

00:23:48.290 --> 00:23:50.510
Again, the website
is upm.mraa.io.

00:23:52.710 --> 00:23:53.460
JOSHUA CHA: Great.

00:23:53.460 --> 00:23:54.880
Thank you, Sundar.

00:23:54.880 --> 00:23:57.500
Number four, how much
does Data Studio cost?

00:23:57.500 --> 00:23:58.970
So there's a theme here.

00:23:58.970 --> 00:24:00.600
Developers are
concerned about cost.

00:24:00.600 --> 00:24:02.016
The good news about
Data Studio is

00:24:02.016 --> 00:24:04.970
that it's a free product as
the date of this recording.

00:24:04.970 --> 00:24:08.420
And it's currently in beta,
so it's a free product.

00:24:08.420 --> 00:24:11.330
However, I do want to inform our
developer community out there

00:24:11.330 --> 00:24:14.630
that if you're using
BigQuery as the data store

00:24:14.630 --> 00:24:16.600
to serve the data
into Data Studio,

00:24:16.600 --> 00:24:19.550
you will be charged for the
querying and the storage

00:24:19.550 --> 00:24:21.740
of that BigQuery
data, so watch that.

00:24:21.740 --> 00:24:25.374
But the product itself
today is a free product.

00:24:25.374 --> 00:24:27.362
SUNDAR SUBRAMANIAM: Cool.

00:24:27.362 --> 00:24:28.330
JOSHUA CHA: Let's see.

00:24:28.330 --> 00:24:28.830
OK.

00:24:28.830 --> 00:24:28.970
All right.

00:24:28.970 --> 00:24:29.469
Great.

00:24:29.469 --> 00:24:32.030
Number five, another Data
Studio related question.

00:24:32.030 --> 00:24:33.200
It's a great question.

00:24:33.200 --> 00:24:35.120
Aside from the built-in
data connectors,

00:24:35.120 --> 00:24:37.850
are there third party connectors
available for Data Studio?

00:24:37.850 --> 00:24:39.350
And the answer is yes.

00:24:39.350 --> 00:24:42.740
If you Google Data Studio
community connectors,

00:24:42.740 --> 00:24:43.880
you'll get a result--

00:24:43.880 --> 00:24:47.090
the first result will be a
page where we allow and educate

00:24:47.090 --> 00:24:50.360
developers to create their
own Data Studio connectors.

00:24:50.360 --> 00:24:53.330
So if the standard built-in
connectors don't do it for you,

00:24:53.330 --> 00:24:54.474
you build your own.

00:24:54.474 --> 00:24:56.390
And you use a product
called App Script, which

00:24:56.390 --> 00:24:59.284
is beyond the scope
of this webinar.

00:24:59.284 --> 00:25:00.950
But it's pretty easy
to pick up as well.

00:25:00.950 --> 00:25:03.380
There's tons of
information online.

00:25:03.380 --> 00:25:05.090
In addition to that
community page,

00:25:05.090 --> 00:25:06.740
we actually have
a gallery as well

00:25:06.740 --> 00:25:08.360
for our third parties
who have already

00:25:08.360 --> 00:25:09.950
created these connectors.

00:25:09.950 --> 00:25:12.240
That gallery is linked
off this community page.

00:25:12.240 --> 00:25:17.270
So again, search for Data
Studio community connectors.

00:25:17.270 --> 00:25:18.770
SUNDAR SUBRAMANIAM: Excellent.

00:25:18.770 --> 00:25:21.420
Here's a question
on device security.

00:25:21.420 --> 00:25:23.450
So are there any
specific tools that

00:25:23.450 --> 00:25:25.700
help increase device security?

00:25:25.700 --> 00:25:27.140
Actually, there are.

00:25:27.140 --> 00:25:30.510
In fact, very recently at
the IoT World Conference,

00:25:30.510 --> 00:25:34.490
we announced a special
feature called a secure device

00:25:34.490 --> 00:25:36.080
onboarding.

00:25:36.080 --> 00:25:38.000
Take a look at it.

00:25:38.000 --> 00:25:41.223
More information is
available at www.intel.com/se

00:25:41.223 --> 00:25:42.467
curitydeviceonboarding.

00:25:45.950 --> 00:25:48.860
With features like
secure device onboarding,

00:25:48.860 --> 00:25:52.460
you can actually onboard
a device securely

00:25:52.460 --> 00:25:54.320
with pretty much zero touch.

00:25:54.320 --> 00:25:57.350
So you don't have to ship
devices with a default

00:25:57.350 --> 00:25:58.940
username and password.

00:25:58.940 --> 00:26:00.810
That's a huge security risk.

00:26:00.810 --> 00:26:04.125
So again, the website
link is www.intel.com/se

00:26:04.125 --> 00:26:07.351
curitydeviceonboarding.

00:26:07.351 --> 00:26:08.100
JOSHUA CHA: Great.

00:26:08.100 --> 00:26:11.150
Another IoT device
related question which is,

00:26:11.150 --> 00:26:13.700
with IoT devices, we
can have thousands

00:26:13.700 --> 00:26:15.110
deployed in the field.

00:26:15.110 --> 00:26:18.770
How many topics and subscribers
can I have in my GCP project?

00:26:18.770 --> 00:26:21.680
So a great question because,
again, with IoT devices

00:26:21.680 --> 00:26:23.740
you tend to work at scale.

00:26:23.740 --> 00:26:24.500
Right?

00:26:24.500 --> 00:26:27.920
So within each project,
each can contain up

00:26:27.920 --> 00:26:29.055
to 10,000 subscribers--

00:26:29.055 --> 00:26:29.930
MARTIN KRONBERG: Wow.

00:26:29.930 --> 00:26:31.263
JOSHUA CHA: --and 10,000 topics.

00:26:31.263 --> 00:26:34.840
So hopefully, that's
enough for your use.

00:26:34.840 --> 00:26:39.240
And lastly, how long will data
be stored in a Pub/Sub topic?

00:26:39.240 --> 00:26:41.850
Well, obviously, acknowledged
messages will be removed

00:26:41.850 --> 00:26:43.240
from the queue immediately.

00:26:43.240 --> 00:26:47.100
However, messages will persist
in that queue for up to seven

00:26:47.100 --> 00:26:48.750
days before they'll be removed.

00:26:48.750 --> 00:26:50.700
So hopefully within
that seven day window,

00:26:50.700 --> 00:26:52.158
you'll be able to
take that Pub/Sub

00:26:52.158 --> 00:26:55.170
data as shown in
the presentation

00:26:55.170 --> 00:26:57.960
and move it into some sort of
persistent storage solution.

00:26:57.960 --> 00:26:58.460
Right?

00:26:58.460 --> 00:27:00.900
Like BigQuery.

00:27:00.900 --> 00:27:03.877
With that, we finished
up the Q&amp;A portion

00:27:03.877 --> 00:27:04.710
